# What this proves, and what it does not:
#
# [V] the plug-in advertises kOfxImageEffectPropOpenCLRenderSupported, keeps the
#     CPU path, and on the CPU path produces the source times two bit for bit
#     through the whole host protocol (118 checks x 3 host profiles).
# [V] with OpenCL enabled it writes through the cl_mem it was handed: the
#     destination buffer starts as a 0x7f seed pattern and the harness only
#     reads a frame back after the queue is finished. A plug-in that wrote to a
#     host pointer instead would leave the seed in place, and the suite says so.
# [V] the device frame is the source times two, bit for bit (max delta 0.0).
# [V] a window smaller than the frame changes only the window: with gain 3.0 on
#     a 5,7..29,31 window, inside is src*3 and outside still src*2 from the
#     previous full-frame render. That is the check that caught the first
#     version of the kernel, which indexed from the buffer origin and rendered
#     the top-left corner for a window somewhere else.
# [V] a negative row pitch is refused (kOfxStatErrUnsupported) instead of walked
#     backwards: ofxImageEffect.h says negative rowBytes is for CPU images only.
# [U] every GPU number. PoCL is a CPU device - this is real driver semantics
#     (kernel build, queue semantics, buffer contents), not hardware throughput.
# [V] the bundle keeps the repository's 2.17 glibc floor. The first build did
#     not: linking -ldl on this glibc 2.34 host recorded dlopen/dlsym/dlerror at
#     GLIBC_2.34, so the gate's floor stage failed the bundle with
#     "needs GLIBC_2.34, bound 2.17" while the other two bundles sat at 2.14.
#     ClRuntime.cpp now asks for dlopen@GLIBC_2.2.5 (and dlsym/dlerror the same
#     way); objdump -T reports GLIBC_2.14 / 2.2.5 / 2.3.4 / 2.4 and
#     tools/check-glibc.sh --max 2.17 passes.
# [U] OpenCL Images mode (kOfxImageEffectPropOpenCLSupported /
#     kOfxImageEffectPropOpenCLImage). The template implements Buffers only, and
#     the harness never hands over a cl_mem in that property.
